  66: def add_attribution(seen, pmid, identity, where, name=None):
  67:     """Identity is recorded WITH ITS KIND, because the kinds are not comparable.
  68: 
  69:     THE FIRST VERSION COMPARED THEM ANYWAY AND MANUFACTURED THREE FALSE ALARMS out of four
  70:     findings. An SSOT object keys a trial by `NCT03315143`; the benchmark file keys the same
  71:     trial by the name `scored`. Those are the SAME TRIAL under two conventions, and a
  72:     detector that calls that a collision is reporting its own inability to join two tables.
  73: 
  74:     A benchmark record carries no NCT id at all, so name-versus-NCT can NEVER match -- which
  75:     means the naive version flags every trial the corpus happens to cite from both sides,
  76:     i.e. exactly the well-recorded ones. It fires hardest where the corpus is most careful.
  77:     NCT-versus-NCT and name-versus-name are comparable and are compared; the cross pair is
  78:     reported as NOT_ASSESSABLE and is never counted as a collision.
  79:     """
